<Base title="Method and corrections" description="The publication's evidence standard, correction ledger and preservation boundaries."><div class="page-heading"><span class="section-number">THE RESEARCH METHOD</span><h1>Keep the finding.<br/><span>Keep its limits.</span></h1><p>An atlas should make a system easier to understand without making the evidence sound stronger than it is.</p></div><div class="method-layout"><nav class="toc" aria-label="Method sections"><a href="#evidence-standard">Evidence standard</a><a href="#corrections">Correction ledger</a><a href="#publication-boundary">Publication boundary</a><a href="#next-questions">Open questions</a></nav><div>
<section class="prose" id="evidence-standard"><h2>Evidence has an environment</h2><p>The original iPod emulator, the partial PinePhone contract model, and the physical PinePhone are separate experimental substrates. Synthetic probes and static inspection supply additional, narrower evidence. Every numbered record names its environment, method, observation and limit.</p><p>Original-client attachment is stronger than a service log. A verified source-to-scanout copy is stronger than a guessed framebuffer pointer. A physical response adds evidence that a model cannot supply. These observations complement one another; none automatically inherits the scope of another.</p><div class="principles"><div><span>01</span><h3>Measure a specific boundary</h3><p>Name the operation and its observable result. Avoid a universal “works” label.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Use a discriminating control</h3><p>A known pattern, a wrong ABI or a missing device should change the expected outcome.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Preserve the counterexample</h3><p>Keep failed and inconclusive experiments separate from the accepted result.</p></div><div><span>04</span><h3>Stop the claim at the evidence</h3><p>A passed workload does not validate an unexercised branch or a different machine.</p></div></div></section>
<section id="corrections" class="corrections"><span class="section-number">THE CORRECTION LEDGER</span><h2>{corrections.length} useful changes of mind.</h2><p>These entries preserve interpretations that the research had to narrow or replace. They are part of the result.</p>{corrections.map((c,i)=><article class="correction"><span class="correction-no">C{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><span class="mono">EARLIER INTERPRETATION</span><h3>{c.belief}</h3><dl><dt>The discriminating test</dt><dd>{c.test}</dd><dt>What changed</dt><dd>{c.result}</dd><dt>The supported statement</dt><dd>{c.replacement}</dd></dl><div class="evidence-links">{c.evidence.map(id=><a href={url('evidence/'+id+'/')}>{id} ↗</a>)}</div></div></article>)}</section>
<section id="publication-boundary" class="prose"><h2>A reference guide, with an explicit boundary</h2><p>This edition is derived from a pinned private project snapshot: <code>{publication.sourceRevision.slice(0,7)}</code>, reviewed on {publication.date}. It contains original explanatory prose, original diagrams, captured-value tables, selected original excerpts, reviewed capture images and public-reference links.</p><p>The implementation repository, firmware, device storage images, extracted artwork, binary modifications, complete raw logs, setup-state procedures and personal data are outside this publication. The repository's MIT license applies to its original material; it does not relicense Apple software or any linked third-party work.</p><p>This is not a complete public reproducibility package. The evidence records report experiments retained privately. Document digests identify what was reviewed, but a digest cannot independently verify an unavailable document's contents. Readers can inspect the selected original lines and fields alongside their interpretation. Omitted material is identified as omitted, and a captured value is not silently reconstructed.</p><p>New work after this snapshot requires a new reviewed record. An in-progress test is not silently included because it appears promising. See <a href={url('sources/')}>sources and provenance</a> for the reference policy.</p></section>
<section id="next-questions" class="prose"><h2>The next questions remain concrete</h2><ul><li>Can listener-aware USB attachment establish ordinary pairing, AFC and repeated replug behavior?</li><li>How broadly do the accepted physical gestures cover keyboard, editing and application behavior?</li><li>What are the 7E18 preview and animation rates under a controlled visible workload? The legacy queue comparison is a separate result.</li><li>Can the sensor path reach accepted stock rotation, and can ordinary restart pass repeated owner hand tests?</li><li>Can actual higher-resolution acquisition and broader gallery behavior retain the cold-power-cycle persistence checks?</li><li>Do CPU compatibility operations preserve atomicity, permissions and copy-on-write under untested cases?</li><li>Can audio, networking and longer power trials reach observable physical acceptance?</li></ul><p>These are research questions at the fixed edition. Read the <a href={url('paper/#11-future-work')}>paper’s future-work argument</a> and <a href={url('claims/')}>claim limits</a> for supporting records.</p><a class="text-link" href={url('articles/preservation-method/')}>Read the full preservation method →</a></section>
</div></div></Base>
